Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Replace markdown tags with HTML tags
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex pattern(R"(^#|##|###|####|#####|######)(\n+$)");
    std::regex_replace(html, pattern, "<h1>$1</h1>");
    std::regex_replace(html, pattern, "<h2>$1</h2>");
    std::regex_replace(html, pattern, "<h3>$1</h3>");
    std::regex_replace(html, pattern, "<h4>$1</h4>");
    std::regex_replace(html, pattern, "<h5>$1</h5>");
    std::regex_replace(html, pattern, "<h6>$1</h6>");
    std::regex_replace(html, pattern, "<h7>$1</h7>");
    return html;
}
```

This function uses regular expressions to replace markdown tags with HTML tags. The pattern matches the markdown tags (`#`, `##`, `###`, etc.) and replaces them with the corresponding HTML tags. The `std::regex_replace` function is used to replace the tags in the input string.

